      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the prevalence and anesthetic impact of obesity in patients undergoing assisted reproductive technologies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective analysis of a complete calendar year of oocyte retrieval procedures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Center for reproductive medicine of a tertiary care university teaching hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "1,289 ASA physical status I, II, and III women undergoing oocyte retrieval procedures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Patient demographics, body mass index (BMI), comorbid conditions, frequency and characterization of intraoperative and postoperative events, route of oocyte retrieval, and anesthetic technique were assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Of the 1,289 women, 33% were overweight or obese. The prevalence of gastroesophageal reflux disease, depression/anxiety, hypothyroidism, diabetes, and hypertension was associated with increasing BMI (P < 0.02). Transvaginal oocyte retrieval and the use of total intravenous anesthesia were less common with increasing BMI (P < 0.01; P < 0.003). Oxygen desaturation occurred more frequently intraoperatively and postoperatively in patients with high BMI (P < 0.0001), as did the reports of postoperative discomfort and the need for additional analgesia (P < 0.001). No patients managed with spinal anesthesia experienced intraoperative desaturation or required conversion to general anesthesia with endotracheal intubation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with high BMI have a greater prevalence of comorbid conditions, require alterations in anesthetic and oocyte retrieval management, and more often experience intraoperative and postoperative events."
        }
      ]},
